ities  in order to  see into the brain in a very different  way  you know one might think that you know  fundamental neuroscience uh is the only  way to go about addressing some of the  issues of the brain initiative and at  doorbot we say yes that is a  foundational science in a foundational  area that should be part of the brain  initiative but there are also other  areas you know other people and again  non-traditional areas that may be able  to provide insight  into the problems associated with the  brain initiative  uh the second component that we feel  very strongly about uh is this aspect we  really want to ensure that the  scientific conversations uh and  endeavors remain very relevant to the  public ultimately it's the taxpayer that  is uh supporting the brain initiative  and we need to make sure that everything  that we deliver back has uh you know  high relevance and high meaning uh to  the general society right so there's a  social contract that goes along with  doing this kind of work and we want to  make sure that we're not just doing  science for the sake of science but are  doing science in order to really again  give back to the people that are  ultimately supporting uh this effort  and then the third part that's extremely  important is that significant innovation  is needed in order to enable these new  opportunities and this really is kind of  boiled down to the hard work right it's  not just talking about the brain  initiative it's not just talking about  the brain brains actually doing that  fundamental science or doing that  fundamental technology transition in  order to make the kinds of things  available um  moving forward into the future so we're  pushing on all of those areas again  working really hard in order to  make those capabilities accessible to  people so they can do great work and  innovate in this space  now at darpa  the people that we serve  are military personnel darpa is a part  of the dod and whenever we think about  the brai