ment the  fingertips of our advanced arm systems  uh with force transducers such that we  can add the sensory component so again  uh technology didn't exist necessarily  to do this on these arm systems so  there's a hardware side that needs to be  built but then the secondary uh more  difficult side is can we put electrode  arrays in the primary sensory cortex  and provide uh inputs into the brain  that would allow somebody to perceive  the sense of touch through the arm  systems so that's the last milestone  that we're trying to achieve on that  program again working really hard uh to  achieve that uh working into the future  okay so that's revolutionizing  prosthetics uh program a lot of uh work  has uh has happened on that thus far  again a few uh last really big  challenges so stay tuned for um uh the  the new developments coming in in that  arena all right  so moving on here  like to talk about the next area of  brain function uh research that we're  working on you remember this progression  we're gonna going from motor control now  to memory restoration okay so uh again  neuroprosthetic control is very  interesting to darpa we think it's a  very new modality for interfacing with  the brain we're doing it for motor kinds  of systems but now the question becomes  for human subjects how do we develop  closed loop you know sensing and  stimulating of the brain to restore the  function to  form and recall memories again for  people living with traumatic brain  injury this is a huge problem  the um the kinds of options that are  available to  restore this function people living with  those disabilities are few and far  between so we're saying can we use  neurotechnology to ultimately do this so  again for the ram program restoring  active memory the vision and goal is to  develop neuroprosthetics for memory  recovery in human subjects living with  this kind of injury now  the ramp program is interesting because  this is darpa's is not our first uh  foray into this  