n we have ucla  sacred university of pennsylvania  michael honda that a little bit  different approaches to this so it's not  freed his focus is really in the  hippocampus and inter rhino cortex again  these are uh very specific areas that  are  involved in the formation  of memory  he's looking at specific types of  modulation of fields  of those neuronal firing patterns that  are involved in memory formation and  recall  and uh and we'll see how his approaches  ultimately pan out  um mike hahn at university of  pennsylvania has a little bit different  approach he's looking at more of the dis  distributed effects  of uh of memory formation and recall so  again we know that the hippocampus and  internal cortex is involved in this but  there are other structures of the brain  that are involved in more of the kind of  consolidation aspects and things like  that so he's taking more of a  distributed approach  to this problem the other really  interesting example or aspect of my  kahana's approach in all this is that  has multiple clinical centers across the  country where he'll be able to  again interact with a large cohort of  human subjects to understand some of  these fundamental fundamental aspects of  memory formation and recall so again  really looking forward to both of these  teams efforts uh just to give you some  background on where they are in their um  efforts uh the ram program started in  july of 2014.  so again we're you know getting close uh  you know to you know eight nine months  of effort on this and these teams are  already in human subjects gathering  their preliminary data developing their  initial devices and again starting their  uh initial analysis on all of that so  again really excited  uh for their work and so please stay  tuned for all of their efforts up moving  forward okay  now uh let's shift gears again here and  start talking about again the next step  in our brain function research portfolio  and let's move into this area i  described