oup of  investigators that know not only basic  uh neurophysiology but also  computational neuroscience  he's teaming up with a small business  called cortera as well as a national  laboratory lawrence livermore national  lab to develop the next generation kind  of device that can again sense from  multiple structures of the brain  uh and on board in that device interpret  that information and then deliver uh  precision stimulation back to the brain  for treating those illnesses so again  fully implantable kind of medical device  for neuropsychiatric illness now one  very interesting aspect of the ucsf  team's approach to this is that they  have a hypothesis okay  that  neuropsychiatric illness  is a condition that uh you know is you  know potentially like evolved over time  okay in in each individual  and that by interacting with brain  circuits in an online and dynamic way  that  neuropsychiatric illness may be  potentially unlearned okay this is a  very profound kind of way of thinking  about neuropsychiatric illness and we  may be able to modulate brain circuits  in such a way that you can unlearn  neuropsychiatric illness  and they have a hypothesis that you know  there's a potential that if that is  indeed true  that while we may initially use a device  like this again to treat the  neuropsychiatric illness maybe some  point in the future that device may be  able to be removed  now again this is very far out kind of a  concept um and you know from darpa's  term we're willing to take a risk on  something like this because it's based  in a very interesting science and it may  work out it may not but along the way we  uh again really feel like we're going to  learn a lot about brain function we're  going to learn a lot about next  generation clinical therapies and a lot  about next generation kind of devices  that could ultimately be used to deliver  that kind of therapy so getting really  excited about that uh again we don't  know if it'll work but again willing to  take the chance on it to see what will  ultimately happen there  uh the second team that i'd like to uh  to tell you about is a team led by imod  escondar again another neuro or a  neurosurgeon again he's at mass general  hospital and has assembled a  a wonderful team of again  neuroscientists computational  neuroscientist as well as hardware  designers to address this problem so  he's teaming up um  with draper laboratories to develop the  hardware  for this kind of uh a problem and the  mgh team has also a very interesting  perspective on neuropsychiatric illness  and close new close-up neuroprosthetics  they have what's called a trans  diagnostic approach so again  neuropsychiatric illness involves many  different structures of the brain there  are many different comorbidities of  neuropsychiatric illness and there are  many different kind of behavioral  expressions  of neuropsychiatric illness and what he  seeks to do is that by using these  closed-loop kind of neural interfaces  can we find the intersections amongst  all of these three kind of axes  of um of neuropsychiatric illness again  traditionally when we think about these  illnesses you think of like that  clinical behavioral diagnostic but we  know that there's much more than that  there's neurophysiology that's involving  these circuits again the behavioral  expression and comorbidities it all  comes together they're trying to link  these aspects uh with their trans  diagnostic approach so again very early  on for both of these teams this project  actually just started in june of 2014  uh they are both both teams are already  in their human clinical tests they're  collecting their initial data sets doing  their initial analysis and developing  their first generation uh kind of  prototype devices so again extremely  excited about the work that's ongoing on  these programs and um you know between  both the ram and the subnets program you  know we we really feel like we're going  to be developing or 