n machine  interfaces and by marrying all of those  aspects together again in awake and  behaving subjects they may be able to  have these kind of real-time  observations of brain structure  activity and behavior all together uh in  a very comprehensive data set and uh  it's it's remarkable the techniques that  this team is uh  is really rolling out uh the neuro fast  program started in uh january of 2014 so  again you know they've been working a  little bit longer and they've developed  some really remarkable kinds of  techniques they have you know real time  uh uh you know calcium reporters uh for  neural activity they're able to capture  in in optically in 3d volumes of tissue  over 800 cells simultaneously have again  real time time series readout from these  optical uh reporters  they have fast laser systems for again  traversing 3d volumes in order to  capture the again the 3d structure and  then they also have the right in  capabilities using optogenetic  techniques  in order to modulate those neurons so  full read write capacity  in this program using optical techniques  and getting awake behaving subjects and  again there's some phenomenal work  that's coming out of both of these  laboratories and you should see some of  those uh those results um  very near in the future  one of the great things about the neuro  fast program is that you know carl and  christian are really pushing the  envelope on this and at darpa we also  like to push the envelope and one of the  aspects that they're also working on in  in conjunction with this work that  they're doing in animal subjects it's  kind of like looking at  excise tissue from human brains and  applying these same kinds of techniques  and looking at 3d volumes of brain  tissue using not only these kind of fast  reporters but also looking at the  using the clarity process as well as  neural stimulation again to have some  really interesting preliminary results  from human neural tissue uh that we can  also in that