ort that next generation or next  kind of  uh ways of doing neuroscience research  into the future so again we're very  proactive to think creatively  about these aspects  in the future  um  next provocation again for uh sharing to  become a priority collaboration must  receive meaningful recognition and  rewards so here's another thing so again  prior to my time at darpa i was an  academic researcher again i've been  through the gauntlet of the 10-year  promotion process so you know i know at  many universities uh across the us as  well as internationally  that you know for 10-year promotion data  sharing and doing science in this way  may not be a priority but again to  address the problems that were really  interested in the future and to really  live up to that social contract maybe it  should be part of that uh tenure and  promotion process so again a provocation  for all of those people that are sitting  on those panels  and thinking about those aspects uh  again moving into the future and again  if we do want to change uh some of those  aspects uh you know we should give  incentives for sharing rewarding teams  and not individuals or maybe uh you know  give incentives for sharing by measuring  impact not just publication so again um  you know  a lot of people think of like  publications and impact factors but what  i'm talking about here is not  necessarily just that is the impact of  neuroscience research on you know  quality of life or  medicine in general again thinking about  the brain initiative as a whole and how  our scientific  contributions can really elevate  all of those kinds of uh of efforts okay  so again the kind of the last  provocation uh that i'd like to leave  with the uh the audience here is that  deliberate growth of a new generation of  hybrid players uh will accelerate  progress again maybe true maybe not true  i don't know but uh it's something that  darpa is very interested in learning  more about this kind of hybrid players  in this 